# Chapter titles are Wisdom's own descriptive English. Sanskrit titles vary by edition, so none is claimed.
# Speakers follow the uvaca markers of the common text: Janaka speaks 1.1 and chapters 2, 4, 6, 7, 12, 13, 14, 19, 20.
JANAKA = {2, 4, 6, 7, 12, 13, 14, 19, 20}
CHAPTERS = {
 1: ("The witness", "Janaka asks how to know, how to be free and how to let go, and Ashtavakra answers that he is already the free witness of everything."),
 2: ("Janaka's wonder", "Janaka finds himself as awareness and bows to it in astonishment, testing the new view against the world, the body and the crowd."),
 3: ("The test", "Ashtavakra tests the king: if you know the Self, why do greed, desire and fear of freedom still move you?"),
 4: ("The knower at play", "The one who knows the Self lives freely, untouched by merit and sin as space is untouched by smoke."),
 5: ("Four ways to rest", "Four short verses, each ending with the same instruction: let the sense of separation dissolve, and rest."),
 6: ("Space, ocean, shell", "Janaka answers that there is nothing to give up, take or dissolve, because the world was never apart from him."),
 7: ("The ship on the ocean", "Janaka watches the world drift like a ship on the ocean of awareness, and feels no need to steer it."),
 8: ("Bondage and freedom", "Four verses that define bondage as the mind wanting and grieving, and freedom as its rest."),
 9: ("Weariness with the world", "Seeing how the opposites never end, one grows tired of chasing them and lets go of every vow."),
 10: ("Quiet", "Leave desire, wealth and even merit; craving is the only bond, and ending it is freedom."),
 11: ("Eight certainties", "Eight verses, each about one who is certain of a single truth and is at peace because of it."),
 12: ("How I came to rest", "Janaka describes how body, speech and thought fell quiet one after another, and how he now rests as he is."),
 13: ("As it pleases me", "Janaka lives without taking or giving up, doing what comes and living at ease."),
 14: ("Peace", "With craving melted, wealth, friends, scripture and learning lose their hold; only those in this state know it."),
 15: ("What you are", "Ashtavakra's second long teaching: you are awareness, the world is waves on your ocean, so move about at ease."),
 16: ("Forget it all", "No scripture or practice brings ease until everything learned is forgotten and effort drops away."),
 17: ("The one who knows", "A portrait of the free person, who wants neither pleasure nor freedom and lives at ease seeing, hearing and eating."),
 18: ("Peace in a hundred verses", "The longest chapter, a third of the text, contrasting the confused who strive with the steady who are already at rest."),
 19: ("My own greatness", "Janaka has pulled the thorn of opinions from his heart; time, place, life and death have no hold on him."),
 20: ("Free while living", "Janaka's last words: in his own nature there is no world, no one seeking freedom, no teacher and no teaching, and nothing rises."),
}
